  "ExplanationCorrect": "The Gordon Growth Model (constant growth DDM) values a stock as the present value of a perpetually growing dividend stream: P₀ = D₁ / (r − g), where D₁ = D₀ × (1 + g) is the NEXT expected dividend, r is the required return, and g is the constant growth rate. D₁ = $2.50 × 1.04 = $2.60. P₀ = $2.60 / (0.11 − 0.04) = $2.60 / 0.07 = $37.14. At the market price of $38.50, the stock trades 3.7% above its intrinsic value under these assumptions. The model is sensitive to the growth rate assumption: if g were 4.5% instead of 4.0%, intrinsic value would be $2.6125 / 0.065 = $40.19 — the stock would be undervalued. This highlights why DDM valuations must be accompanied by sensitivity analysis. The required return (11.00%) must exceed the growth rate (4.00%) — if g ≥ r, the model produces nonsensical (negative or infinite) values.",
