  "ExplanationCorrect": "A forward hedge eliminates transaction exposure by locking in today the exchange rate at which future foreign-currency-denominated payables or receivables will be settled. Forward hedge cost = Forward rate × Foreign currency amount = $1.135/€ × €2,000,000 = $2,270,000 in 60 days. A money market hedge replicates the forward contract synthetically: (1) buy the present value of €2,000,000 today at the euro borrowing rate, (2) invest those euros to grow to exactly €2,000,000 in 60 days to meet the payable, and (3) finance the euro purchase with a dollar loan. Under covered interest rate parity, the forward rate = Spot × (1 + r_domestic) / (1 + r_foreign), so the two methods should produce equivalent costs in efficient markets. Systematic differences arise only from transaction costs or deviations from parity. The forward hedge has the advantage of simplicity — one transaction fixes the exchange rate with certainty. The money market hedge involves multiple transactions (borrowing, converting, investing) and may have higher transaction costs.",
