  "ExplanationCorrect": "Portfolio beta is the weighted average of individual security betas, weighted by the proportion of portfolio value invested in each security. Portfolio value = $300K + $200K = $500K. Weight of X = $300K/$500K = 0.60; weight of Y = $200K/$500K = 0.40. Portfolio beta = (0.60 × 1.40) + (0.40 × 0.70) = 0.84 + 0.28 = 1.12. This means the portfolio moves 1.12% for every 1% move in the market. The critical conceptual distinction: systematic risk (beta) reflects market-wide factors affecting all securities — it cannot be diversified away. Unsystematic risk (firm-specific factors) CAN be reduced through diversification. Adding Stock Z with a beta of 1.80 would INCREASE the portfolio beta (raising systematic risk exposure) while potentially reducing unsystematic risk. Diversification reduces unsystematic risk; it does not and cannot reduce systematic (market) risk.",
